{The concept of color gamut refers to the range of colors that a particular device can produce or record.}
{A color gamut is typically shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.}
{The most commonly known color gamut is "sRGB," which was established by the International Electrotechnical Commission in 1999.}
{To achieve color fidelity or color management, it's crucial to ensure that all devices utilize the same RGB color coordinates.}
{It's essential to understand the differences between various color gamuts, such as NTSC, AdobeRGB, DCI-P3, and others, based on their intended applications and characteristics.}
